<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunsrück</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hunsrück is a low mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> and the Saar. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a patchwork of small lordships, among them the counties of Sponheim and Veldenz, the lands of the Wild- and Rhinegraves and the Palatine line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a>. Its hilly, wooded landscape became widely known through Edgar Reitz's film series Heimat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunsruck.jpg" alt="Hunsrück" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was ErnstA at German Wikipedia. Later versions were uploaded by Roßbacher at de.wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HunsrueckLage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Hunsrück is a long, triangular upland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, bounded by the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> and Saar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahe">Nahe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and a school rhyme teaches children its limits: Moselle, Nahe, Saar and Rhine enclose our Hunsrück. It continues as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> beyond the Rhine and as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> beyond the Moselle, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> lies at its north-eastern corner. The range is around 100 km long and 25 to 30 km wide. Its interior is rarely higher than 450 metres, and its highest peak is the Erbeskopf, at 816 metres. The Idar Forest, the Soonwald and the Bingen Forest form an almost unbroken belt of forest, and the climate brings frequent morning mists and more rain than the German average.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Stone axes show that the Hunsrück has been settled since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">New Stone Age</a>, and Gravettian sites mark the first settlements in the area. In the La Tène period the Hunsrück-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> culture, linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, left the Waldalgesheim prince's grave, the circular rampart of Otzenhausen and the Pfalzfeld obelisk. The region was then the tribal area of the Treveri. Between about 50 BCE and 400 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> opened it up with a dense network of roads, the best known being the Via Ausonia, which linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> with Bingen.</p><p>The name Hundesrucha is first mentioned in a deed of 1074 from Ravengiersburg Abbey. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the land was fragmented among the counts Palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, the counts of Sponheim and the successors of the Emichones, the Wildgraves, the Raugraves and the counts of Veldenz. Many castles and customs stations were built between the 11th and mid 14th century. The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> emerged in 1410 under a side line of the counts Palatine. In 1689 French troops set fire to Kirchberg, Kastellaun, Simmern and Stromberg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>.</p><p>After the French period, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave most of the Hunsrück to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s Rhine Province in 1815. Poor harvests and the famine of 1817 followed, and in 1823 the agent Georg Anton Schäffer, sent by Emperor Dom Pedro I of Brazil, recruited many emigrants there. The first settled near São Leopoldo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> in 1824, and further waves left in the 1840s and 1861, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The notable towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a>, Kirchberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idar-oberstein">Idar-Oberstein</a>, Kastellaun and Morbach, and Frankfurt-Hahn Airport lies at the centre of the upland. Slate is still mined there. The region is best known culturally for the Hunsrückisch dialect and for its depiction in the Heimat film series.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hunsrück” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Electoral Palatinate was a fragmented territory of scattered districts on both banks of the Rhine, from the <b>Hunsrück</b> to the Odenwald, interspersed with bishoprics, imperial cities and lesser counties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts of Sponheim appear in 1044 near the Nahe in the <b>Hunsrück</b>, with their castle at Sponheim and a family monastery founded there.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-sponheim">County of Sponheim</a></li><li><blockquote>Their lands lay scattered in the <b>Hunsrück</b>, along the Moselle and in the western Palatinate around Meisenheim and Lauterecken.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-veldenz">County of Veldenz</a></li><li><blockquote>It kept the detached principalities of Lübeck around Eutin and Birkenfeld in the <b>Hunsrück</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-oldenburg">Free State of Oldenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1569 Duke Wolfgang of Palatinate-Zweibrücken divided his lands among his sons, and Charles I received Birkenfeld, part of the old County of Sponheim in the <b>Hunsrück</b>, founding the line of Palatinate-Birkenfeld.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-birkenfeld">Palatinate-Birkenfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>The Simmern dukes ruled a hilly territory in the <b>Hunsrück</b> around Simmern together with their share of Sponheim.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a></li></ul></section>
